# Does an OZ simple_threshold restore m-of-n beside our interpreter?
# Generated 2026-08-23T04:08:51Z by packages/policy-synth/scripts/oz-threshold-binding.ts
#
# "Two signers must approve every transfer" is NOT the default on an OZ smart
# account, and the naive attempt gives the OPPOSITE. A no-policy context rule
# requires the FULL signer set (all-of-N), but attaching ANY policy defers
# signer validation to the policy, so a policed rule lets any ONE signer act
# alone. simple_threshold is what counts signatures back:
# `authenticated_signers.len() >= threshold` else #3202.
#
# One rule shape throughout - TWO signers (A and B) on BOTH rules. Only the
# extra policy varies:
#   rule 1  CONTROL  interpreter only
#   rule 2  SUBJECT  interpreter + simple_threshold(2)
#
# Three calls, each load-bearing:
#   1. CONTROL  A alone        -> PASS. This IS the any-of-N inversion, shown
#                                 rather than asserted, and it is what
#                                 attributes the deny below to the threshold.
#   2. SUBJECT  A alone        -> DENY #3202 SimpleThresholdError::NotAllowed
#   3. SUBJECT  A + B together -> PASS. A rule that refuses everything would
#                                 not be a threshold.
#
# simple_threshold is OZ example code we built from tag v0.7.2 and deployed
# ourselves. We did not audit it; see docs/audit/README.md finding 7.

$ bun packages/policy-synth/scripts/oz-threshold-binding.ts --network testnet
